### 2018A&A...617A.120M: Tracing the cold and warm physico-chemical structure of deeply embedded protostars: IRAS 16293-2422 vs. VLA 1623-2417 (2018; star_formation, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What factors determine the cold and warm chemical structure of two Class 0 protostars, IRAS 16293-2422 and VLA 1623-2417, as seen in key molecular tracers?
- step 2: Model DCO+ peak position with chemical network
  - method: Steady-state analytic H2D+/DCO+ network with CO freeze-out drop abundance, passed through RATRAN to synthetic cubes, temperature/density profiles scaled by arbitrary factors [4.1.1: "a simple steady-state, analytic chemical network that accounts for the basic reactions leading to the production and destruction of is used."]
  - software: RATRAN
  - parameter: T_sub = 20–40 K, n_de = 1e5–1e8 cm-3, X[CO] = 1e-7–1e-4 [4.1.1: "we explore the parameter ranges of $T_{\rm sub}$ = 20–40 K, $n_{\rm de}$ = 10$^{5}$–10$^{8}$ cm$^{-3}$ and $X[\ce{CO}]$ = 10$^{-7}$ – 10$^{-4}$."]
  - assumption: cosmic-ray ionization rate 1.26e-17 s-1; ortho-to-para H2 lower limit 1e-3; sources centred on and dominated by A [4.1.1: "The ortho-to-para ratio is set to have a lower limit of 10$^{-3}$ at low temperatures"]
  - fitting: By-eye fit of radial profile [4.1.1: "A by eye fit of the chemical model to the observations is used to constrain the decrease in the temperature profile"]
  - validation: Consistency with DCO+ results of VLA 1623 in Murillo et al. 2015 and with line-ratio temperatures [4.1.2: "This temperature is consistent with the chemical modelling of the peak position."]
- step 3: Derive T and n from line ratios (DCO+, c-C3H2)
  - method: RADEX non-LTE calculations compared to observed ratios, with peak intensities converted from Jy/beam to K [4.1.2: "Using RADEX (cite: vandertak2007), we performed non-LTE excitation and radiative transfer calculations to constrain the temperature and density"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: IRAS 16293: N=3e12 cm-2, width 1.0 km/s; VLA 1623: N=2e12 cm-2, width 0.7 km/s [4.1.2: "We adopt a line width of 1.0 km s$^{-1}$ and a column density of 3 $\times$ 10$^{12}$ cm$^{-2}$"]
  - assumption: ortho/para ratio of 3 for c-C3H2; molecular data from LAMDA [4.2: "To convert to the total (ortho + para) column density, an o/p ratio of 3 was used."]
  - validation: APEX single-dish ratio used as cross-check for VLA 1623 [4.1.2: "The APEX observations are used to double check if this is the kinetic temperature of the bulk of the emission at the disk-envelope interface."]
- step 4: Test non-detection of N-bearing lines in VLA 1623
  - method: Predict ALMA peak intensities from single-dish detections via RADEX for extended and compact (1") emission [4.4: "Two cases are explored, extended and compact emission concentrated in a 1$\ensuremath{''}$ region."]
  - software: RADEX
- result: Temperature reduction factor, VLA 1623 = 1.5 ± 0.2 dimensionless [4.1.1: "The factor is constrained to be 1.5+/-0.2 for VLA 1623 and 3.0+/-0.2 for IRAS 16293."]
- result: Temperature reduction factor, IRAS 16293 = 3.0 ± 0.2 dimensionless [4.1.1: "The factor is constrained to be 1.5+/-0.2 for VLA 1623 and 3.0+/-0.2 for IRAS 16293."]
- result: DCO+ 5-4/3-2 ratio, IRAS 16293 = 0.6 ± 0.1 dimensionless [4.1.2: "a ratio 5–4/3–2 = 0.6 $\pm$ 0.1 is obtained from the PILS Band 7 observations and the SMA 230 GHz observations"]
- result: Kinetic temperature from DCO+, IRAS 16293 = 20–23 ± not stated K [4.1.2: "a kinetic temperature between 20 and 23 K can be inferred for IRAS 16293"]
- result: DCO+ 5-4/3-2 ratio, VLA 1623 ALMA = 1.0 ± 0.1 dimensionless [4.1.2: "the ALMA 12m array observations provide 5–4/3–2 = 1.0 $\pm$ 0.1"]
- result: Kinetic temperature from DCO+, VLA 1623 ALMA = 30–55 ± not stated K [4.1.2: "This line brightness temperature ratio implies a kinetic temperature between 30 and 55 K"]
- result: DCO+ ratio, VLA 1623 APEX = 0.2 ± 0.03 dimensionless [4.1.2: "The APEX data give a much lower line ratio, 5–4/3–2 = 0.2 $\pm$ 0.03."]
- result: Kinetic temperature, VLA 1623 APEX = 12–19 ± not stated K [4.1.2: "well reproduced by a kinetic temperature of 12 to 19 K"]
- result: Kinetic temperature at c-C3H2 south peak, IRAS 16293 = 120–155 ± not stated K [4.2: "We find that the temperature for the south peak, corresponding to the outflow cavity of IRAS 16293 A , is between 120–155 K."]
