### 2021A&A...652A...5M: A study of submillimeter methanol absorption toward PKS 1830‒211:. Excitation, invariance of the proton-electron mass ratio, and systematics (2021; astrochemistry, cosmology, agn_and_quasars; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Constrain the excitation of submillimeter methanol absorption lines at z=0.89 toward PKS 1830-211 and test the cosmological invariance of the proton-electron mass ratio mu from the relative kinematics of the lines
- step 4: Constrain methanol excitation with a RADEX non-LTE grid
  - method: RADEX models on a grid of n_H2 and Tkin (also G1 column density and A/E), compared to the ALMA spectra via chi2 summed over tunings; two Gaussian components treated as independent [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "We adopted a uniform sphere geometry and took the two-Gaussian line profile (i.e., fixing their velocity centroids, line widths, and integrated opacity ratios)"] [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "For each model, we constructed the RADEX methanol spectrum and calculated the resulting $\chi^2$, summing over the different tunings $i$ as"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: Grid: n_H2 1e3-1e6 cm^-3, Tkin 6-100 K [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "We explored a density range between $10^3$ and $10^6$ cm$^{-3}$ (in logarithmic steps) and kinetic temperatures between 6 and 100 K"]
  - parameter: Two B4 visits averaged, average f_c adopted; 13CH3OH modeled with ratio 62 but not in the chi2 [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "For this exercise, the two visits of the B4 tuning were averaged, and we took their average $f_c$."]
  - assumption: Para-H2 is the collisional partner; no additional heating by ambient radiation field or cosmic rays; T_CMB = 5.14 K [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "Para-H$_2$ is assumed to be the main collisional partner, since it is expected to be the dominant form at low temperatures ($T < 100$ K)."] [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "we did not consider additional heating, either by the ambient radiative field or cosmic rays"]
  - fitting: Grid chi2 minimization; 1-sigma contour defined at Min(chi2_r)+2.30 [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "The equivalent $1\sigma$ contour, defined at a value Min($\chi^2_r)+2.30$"]
  - validation: Good match for A-CH3OH; E-CH3OH lines (except ground state) underestimated; removing 3_0-2_-1 and 4_0-3_-1 E lowers chi2_r from 13 to 9 [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "for most lines of E-CH$_3$OH (except the ground state transition), the RADEX spectra underestimate the absorption depths"]
- step 5: Derive A/E ratio, methanol abundance and 12C/13C ratio
  - method: A/E from the RADEX grid; total column from summing A and E over G1 and G2 divided by an H2 proxy; 12CH3OH/13CH3OH from the tied opacity ratio in the joint profile fit [5.2 Methanol relative abundance: "By adding the column densities of A- and E-CH$_3$OH over the two velocity components $G_1$ and $G_2$ , we estimate a total methanol column density of $\sim 5 \times 10^{14}$ cm$^{-2}$ along the southwest line of sight."] [5.3 The 12CH3OH/13CH3OH ratio: "we tied the opacities of the $^{13}$CH$_3$OH lines to those corresponding to the $^{12}$C- main isotopologue, considering only the two brightest ones"]
  - software: RADEX
  - assumption: H2 column density 2e22 cm^-2 from 2012 CH spectra used as a proxy, despite profile variations between 2012 and 2019 [5.2 Methanol relative abundance: "We further take the H$_2$ column density of $2 \times 10^{22}$ cm$^{-2}$, estimated by (cite: mul14a) using CH spectra (obtained in 2012), as an H$_2$ proxy."]
- result: Best-fit RADEX Tkin and n_H2 (chi2_r = 13) = Tkin = 9 K; n_H2 = 1.3e5 cm^-3; A/E = 1 ± 1-sigma region Tkin 8-17 K, n_H2 2e4-2e5 cm^-3 K; cm^-3 [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "an A/E abundance ratio of 1, $\hbox{$T_\mathrm{kin}$}=9$ K, and $\hbox{$n_\mathrm{H_2}$} = 1.3 \times 10^5$ cm$^{-3}$"]
- result: Best-fit A-CH3OH column densities for G1 and G2 = 1.8e14 (G1) and 0.7e14 (G2) ± not stated cm^-2 [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "column densities of A-CH$_3$OH of $1.8 \times 10^{14}$ cm$^{-2}$ and $0.7 \times 10^{14}$ cm$^{-2}$ for $G_1$ and $G_2$, respectively"]
- result: Methanol A/E abundance ratio = 1.0 ± 0.1 dimensionless [5.1 The A/E ratio: "we estimate an abundance ratio A/E $= 1.0 \pm 0.1$"]
- result: Total methanol column density (southwest) = ~5e14 ± not stated cm^-2 [5.2 Methanol relative abundance: "we estimate a total methanol column density of $\sim 5 \times 10^{14}$ cm$^{-2}$ along the southwest line of sight"]
- result: CH3OH/H2 abundance (southwest) = ~2e-8 ± not stated (rough estimate) dimensionless [5.2 Methanol relative abundance: "This yields an average methanol abundance relative to H$_2$ of CH$_3$OH/H$_2 \sim 2 \times 10^{-8}$ toward the southwest image"]
- result: 12CH3OH/13CH3OH ratio = 62 ± 3 dimensionless [5.3 The 12CH3OH/13CH3OH ratio: "ratio of $62 \pm 3$"]
- limitation (authors): Uncertainties in the four-dimensional RADEX parameter space are not straightforward to define [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "It is not straightforward to define uncertainties in the four-dimensional parameter space."]
- limitation (authors): RADEX underestimates absorption depths for most E-CH3OH lines [5 Excitation of methanol lines: "for most lines of E-CH$_3$OH (except the ground state transition), the RADEX spectra underestimate the absorption depths"]
- limitation (authors): Methanol abundance is a rough estimate because H2 column is from 2012 and profile varied [5.2 Methanol relative abundance: "We note that this is a rough estimate, because there were some variations in the line profile between 2012 and 2019"]
- not stated (card): CASA version used for calibration, mstransform and UVMultiFit; Whether the standard ALMA pipeline or manual scripts were used, and any flagging; Continuum normalization and how the 'normalized to continuum' spectra were produced from the UVMultiFit output (fit parameters, spectral windows, line-free channels); Exact two-Gaussian fit initial values and velocity windows/channel ranges used for each…

