### 2014ApJ...785..149S: The Rest-frame Submillimeter Spectrum of High-redshift, Dusty, Star-forming Galaxies (2014; high_z_galaxies, astrochemistry; archival_reuse)
- question: What is the average rest-frame 250-770 GHz spectrum of lensed SPT dusty star-forming galaxies, and what ISM conditions do its faint lines (13CO, HCN, HNC, HCO+, CN) imply?
- step 4: Excitation modelling of 12CO/13CO and dense-gas tracers
  - method: RADEX grid models (expanding sphere) fit to line ratios with 15% added uncertainty; priors tau<100 and virialized K_vir [5 Results: "line optical depths are constrained to be $\tau < 100$"]
  - software: RADEX, LAMDA
  - parameter: N_CO/dv=1e15-1e19, Tkin=12-300 K, nH2=10-1e6, 12CO/13CO=10-1e4; TCMB~11 K [5 Results: "Our grid of models spans $N_\mathrm{CO} / \Delta v = 10^{15}-10^{19}$ $\rm{cm}^{-2}(\rm{km}/\rm{s})^{-1}$, $\ensuremath{T_\mathrm{kin}}= 12-300$ K"]
  - assumption: single homogeneous gas component; NGC253 abundance ratios for dense tracers [5 Results: "we assume the relative abundance ratios of CN, HCN, HNC, and $\rm{HCO}^+$ are the same as in the local starburst galaxy NGC253"]
  - fitting: Grid chi-square with posterior distributions; minimum chi2=10.4 for 3 dof [5 Results: "We find that no single model fits all seven available line ratios well (minimum $\chi^2 = 10.4$ for three degrees of freedom)."]
  - validation: Appendix test recovering representative conditions from mock SLEDs of 22 sources [Appendix B: "conditions representative of the inputs are recovered despite the variety of conditions used to generate the input spectra."]
- result: Dense gas kinetic temperature = ~55 ± not stated K [5.2: "with $\ensuremath{T_\mathrm{kin}}\sim 55$ K and $\ensuremath{n_\mathrm{H_2}}\sim 10^{5.5}-10^{7.5}$ $\rm{cm}^{-3}$"]
- result: 12CO/13CO abundance ratio for Td-coupled gas = ~100-200 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1: "an abundance ratio \[$^{12}\rm{CO}$/$^{13}\rm{CO}$\]$\sim 100-200$"]
- limitation (authors): Single-component modelling of a heterogeneous sample; no single model fits (chi2 min 10.4 for 3 dof); degeneracies remain; differential magnification not accounted [4.1: "This likely reflects the simplicity of the modeling procedure, which represents a heterogeneous collection of galaxies as a single, homogeneous gas component of simple geometry."]
- not stated (card): Calibrator names and flux scale uncertainty of the Cycle 0 data; CASA version and exact calibration scripts; Per-source SED fit parameters and photometry used for 1.4 mm scaling; Exact interpolation scheme onto the 500 km/s grid; RADEX grid sampling steps, likelihood and posterior/marginalisation details, K_vir cutoff; Source coordinates; Which sources are included for each line, beyond N_sources…

### 2019ApJ...880....2W: Spatially Resolved Interstellar Medium and Highly Excited Dense Molecular Gas in the Most Luminous Quasar at z = 6.327 (2019; agn_and_quasars, high_z_galaxies; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the host-galaxy ISM, gas excitation, dust properties and kinematics of the most luminous z=6.327 quasar J0100+2802, from sub-kpc ALMA imaging of [CII], CO(6-5), continuum and high-J CO?
- step 4: Model CO SLED
  - method: RADEX single and two-component LVG; chi2 for single, MCMC for two components [4.1 CO Spectral Line Energy Distribution: "we performed the Markov chain Monte Carlo (cite: mcmc) calculations to fit our observed fluxes with the fluxes generated from RADEX models"]
  - software: RADEX, emcee
  - parameter: n(H2)=10^2-10^7 cm-3, Tkin=Tcmb-1000 K, N_CO/dv=10^15.5-10^19.5 [4.1 CO Spectral Line Energy Distribution: "$n_{\rm (H_2)}$=10$^2$–10$^7$ cm$^{-3}$, T$_{\rm kin}$=T$_{\rm CMB}$-10$^3$ K"]
  - assumption: background temperature = CMB at z=6.327 [4.1 CO Spectral Line Energy Distribution: "We set the background temperature to be the CMB temperature at $z=6.327$."]
  - validation: single-component reduced chi2=2 does not reproduce SLED shape [4.1 CO Spectral Line Energy Distribution: "the best-fit single LVG model does not reproduce the shape of CO SLED well"]
- result: CO SLED cool component = T_kin=24 K, n=10^4.5 cm-3 ± T +8/-3; logn ±1.1 K; cm-3 [4.1 CO Spectral Line Energy Distribution: "$n_{\rm (H_2)}$=10$^{4.5_{-1.1}^{+1.1}}$ cm$^{-3}$ and T$_{\rm kin}$=24$_{-3}^{+8}$ K for the cool component"]
- result: CO SLED warm component = T_kin=224 K, n=10^3.6 cm-3 ± T +165/-100 K; cm-3 [4.1 CO Spectral Line Energy Distribution: "$n_{\rm (H_2)}$=10$^{3.60_{-0.8}^{+1.3}}$ cm$^{-3}$ and T$_{\rm kin}$=224$_{-100}^{+165}$ K for the warm component"]
- limitation (authors): Continuum points lie on the Rayleigh-Jeans tail, so T_dust and beta are degenerate; shorter-wavelength data needed. [3.1 Dust Temperature and Emissivity Ind…: "most of the continuum points are on the Rayleigh–Jeans tail of the dust emission and we cannot tightly constrain the temperature and emissivity at the same time"]
- not stated (card): Line-free channel ranges for continuum maps and uvcontsub fit order; Image size and cell size, clean threshold, masks, number of iterations; Robust values for the CO(7-6), CO(10-9), CO(11-10) cubes; CASA version and pipeline/manual calibration details; Flux calibrator flux scale uncertainty; Spectral resolution/channel width of CO cubes; MCMC settings and priors for the RADEX fit; chi2 uncertaint…

### 2020ApJ...889L...4L: Revealing the CO X-factor in Dark Molecular Gas through Sensitive ALMA Absorption Observations (2020; milky_way_ism, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the properties of CO and HCO+ in diffuse, cold dark molecular gas, and what is the CO X-factor there, as probed by absorption toward continuum sources?
- step 3: RADEX modeling for Tex and column densities
  - method: Chi2 minimisation over n_H2, N_CO, N_HCO+ fitting four spectra toward 3C454.3; other sources use n_H2 from 3C454.3 [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "we vary the free parameters $\mathrm{n_{H_2}}$, $\mathrm{N_{CO}}$, and $\mathrm{N_{HCO^+}}$ to find the optimum solutions by minimizing $\chi^2$"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: Tkin = 100 K [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "Adopting a kinetic temperature of 100 K for the diffuse ISM"]
  - fitting: chi2 minimisation [Radiative transfer analysis: "we vary the free parameters $\mathrm{n_{H_2}}$, $\mathrm{N_{CO}}$, and $\mathrm{N_{HCO^+}}$ to find the optimum solutions by minimizing $\chi^2$"]
  - validation: Varying Tkin 10-300 K changes Tex and N by <1% (CO), 0.1% (HCO+) [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "By varying the assumed $\mathrm{T_{kin}}$ value from 10 to 300 K, we found that the fractional difference are within 1$\%$ for $\mathrm{T_{ex} (CO)}$ and $\mathrm{N_{CO}}$"]
- result: Tex CO toward 3C454.3 = 4.1 ± not stated K [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "The derived excitation temperature values are 4.1 K for CO and 2.7 K for HCO$^+$."]
- result: Tex HCO+ toward 3C454.3 = 2.7 ± not stated K [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "The derived excitation temperature values are 4.1 K for CO and 2.7 K for HCO$^+$."]
- result: n_H2 toward 3C454.3 = 87 ± not stated cm^-3 [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "The best fit values for $\mathrm{n_{H_2}}$, $\mathrm{N_{CO}}$, and $\mathrm{N_{HCO^+}}$ toward 3C454.3 are 87 $\mathrm{cm^{-3}}$, 3.6$\times 10^{14}$ $\mathrm{cm^{-2}}$, and 1.8$\times 10^{11}$ $\mathrm{cm^{-2}}$"]
- result: N_CO toward 3C454.3 = 3.6e14 ± not stated cm^-2 [4 Radiative transfer analysis: "3.6$\times 10^{14}$ $\mathrm{cm^{-2}}$"]
- limitation (authors): HI column densities are lower limits for most sightlines (optically thin assumption) [5 HCO+ as a molecular gas tracer: "for the majority of sightlines represents a lower limit under the optically thin assumption."]
- not stated (card): Robust value, cell size, image size, masking for imaging; Spectral extraction method and continuum-free channels; Number of Gaussian components and fitting priors/initial guesses; RADEX grid ranges and collision-rate files; How W_CO was reconstructed from tau and Tex (integration limits); Which sources enter the X-factor average; Beam size, rms in K, CASA/pipeline version; Source coordinates and …

